## Rule 2 — Core Commands

Android CLI replaces a long tail of bespoke `gradlew` / `adb` / `avdmanager` / `sdkmanager` invocations with a single typed surface. Prefer these over raw shell.

```bash
# Build
android build                            # debug, default variant
android build --variant release          # release APK
android build --module :feature:home     # build a single module
android build --aab                      # Android App Bundle for Play

# Run
android run --device pixel-8-api-35      # build, install, launch on a target device
android run --variant debug --keep-data  # preserve app data between runs

# Test
android test                             # unit + Compose UI tests for all modules
android test --module :feature:home      # one module
android test --device emulator           # instrumented tests on the default emulator

# Describe — emit structured project metadata for grounding an agent
android describe                         # human-readable summary on stdout
android describe --format json           # JSON payload for tools and hooks
android describe --output .android/describe.json

# Docs — fetches official Android documentation by query (cached locally)
android docs "Navigation 3 entry providers"
android docs "Compose Modifier.semantics"
android docs --offline "BroadcastReceiver registerReceiver flags"

# Emulator
android emulator list                    # available AVDs + running instances
android emulator create pixel-8-api-35   # creates a new AVD with sane defaults
android emulator start pixel-8-api-35    # boots headlessly by default
android emulator stop pixel-8-api-35
```

**Token economy:** `android describe --format json` produces a compact, deduplicated project map — modules, source sets, AGP/Kotlin versions, applied skills, journeys, and exposed app functions — typically **<2 KB** versus the **30 KB+** an agent would scrape by reading every `build.gradle.kts`. Always prefer `describe` over file-by-file inspection when grounding context.

