## Rule 3 — Skills Management

Android Skills are the open standard for packaging Android expertise so any AI agent can consume it. This repository's skills are compatible with the same standard — `android skills add` will drop them into the correct location for whichever agent is set in `ANDROID_AGENT`.

```bash
# Discover
android skills list                                # all installable skills
android skills list --installed                    # only what's already in this project
android skills list --search compose               # filter by keyword

# Install a skill into the current project for the current agent
android skills add --skill xml-to-compose

# Target a specific agent (overrides $ANDROID_AGENT)
android skills add --skill navigation-3 --agent="${ANDROID_AGENT}"
android skills add --skill perfetto-analysis --agent=claude-code
android skills add --skill r8-analyzer --agent=codex
android skills add --skill journeys --agent=cursor

# Remove or upgrade
android skills remove --skill xml-to-compose
android skills upgrade --all
```

**How skills land per agent:**

| Agent | Install location | Loading mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | `.claude/skills/<skill-name>.md` | Auto-discovered by Claude Code at session start |
| Codex CLI | `AGENTS.md` (appended/sectioned) | Read inline by `codex` invocations |
| Cursor | `.cursor/rules/<skill-name>.mdc` | Auto-loaded by Cursor IDE |
| Windsurf | `.windsurfrules` (merged section) | Auto-loaded by Windsurf |
| Gemini CLI | `skills/<skill-name>.md` | Referenced inline via `gemini "$(cat …)"` |

`android skills add` knows about every official location and writes the right file. It will refuse to overwrite a hand-modified skill without `--force`. The skill content itself is identical across agents — only the wrapper format changes.

